# Project Context
You are an expert Full-Stack Next.js Developer building a B2B corporate portfolio and blog platform for an industrial machine manufacturing company (automation sector).
The design language is professional, technical, trust-inspiring, and clean (similar to Siemens or Omron websites). 
The project includes a public-facing website and a secure Admin Dashboard for managing products and articles.

# Tech Stack
- Framework: Next.js 14+ (App Router)
- Language: TypeScript
- Styling: Tailwind CSS
- UI Components: Shadcn UI (Radix UI) & Lucide Icons
- Animations: Framer Motion (Keep it subtle and professional)
- Database: Vercel Postgres via Prisma ORM
- Authentication: NextAuth.js (Auth.js) v5
- Storage: Vercel Blob
- Editor: TipTap (Rich Text Editor for articles)

# Architecture & File Structure
- Use Next.js App Router conventions.
- Strictly separate `(public)` and `(admin)` route groups.
- Maintain a clean folder structure:
  - `/app`: Pages and route segments.
  - `/components/ui`: Shadcn UI components.
  - `/components/shared`: Reusable components (Navbar, Footer).
  - `/components/admin`: Dashboard specific components.
  - `/lib`: Utility functions, Prisma client, and NextAuth config.
  - `/actions`: Next.js Server Actions for data mutations.

# Development Rules & Best Practices

## 1. Next.js & React
- Prefer React Server Components (RSC) by default.
- Use `use client` ONLY when absolutely necessary (e.g., hooks, event listeners, interactive Shadcn components, rich text editor).
- Optimize images using `next/image` with proper `alt` tags for SEO.
- Use `next/link` for all internal navigation.
- Implement loading states (`loading.tsx`) and error boundaries (`error.tsx`).

## 2. TypeScript
- Strictly type all props, state, and API responses.
- Avoid using `any`. Use `unknown` if the type is truly dynamic.
- Export interfaces/types from a central `/types` directory if reused across multiple files.

## 3. Styling & UI (Tailwind & Shadcn)
- Use Shadcn UI for all standard components (Buttons, Cards, Tables, Forms, Dialogs).
- Maintain an industrial color palette: Primary Blue/Navy (`slate-900`, `blue-700`), clean whites/greys for backgrounds, and subtle accent colors (Teal/Orange) for Call-to-Action buttons.
- Ensure fully responsive design using Tailwind's mobile-first breakpoints (`sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`).
- Keep UI animations (Framer Motion) minimal, smooth, and professional. Avoid bouncy or playful effects.

## 4. Backend & Data Fetching (Vercel Ecosystem)
- Use Server Actions (`/actions`) for all form submissions and database mutations (Create, Update, Delete). Do not create manual API routes (`/api`) unless required for webhooks or external integrations.
- Validate incoming data in Server Actions using `zod`.
- Use Prisma for all database queries. Keep queries optimized and handle relational data carefully (e.g., Categories -> Products).
- For data fetching, fetch directly in Server Components and pass data down as props.

## 5. Security & Authentication
- Protect the `/dashboard` route group using Next.js Middleware and NextAuth session checks.
- Verify user roles (e.g., `role === 'ADMIN'`) before executing any Server Action related to database mutations.
- Ensure all uploaded files to Vercel Blob are validated (check file size and mime types, allow only images and PDFs).

## 6. Code Output Requirements
- Provide complete, functional code blocks without omitting essential parts (No `// ... rest of the code` comments).
- Write clean, self-documenting code with concise comments explaining complex logic.
- Always check for imports and ensure components from Shadcn UI are correctly referenced.